5. Conclusion
To investigate the effects of channel shape, spiral angle, fluid velocity, and fluid pressure on pressure losses, hydraulic tests were extensively carried out. Two cylinders with five different cooling channels of 15 ̊ and 30 ̊ spiral angles were manufactured using end mill tools. The heights and widths of the channels perpendicular to the flow direction were 1.5/2.0/2.5 mm and 1.0/2.0/3.0 mm, respectively. The flow velocities and pressures were 10/20/30 m/s and 10/30/50 bar. The friction coefficients were obtained from the present experimental data and were compared with those from the previous empirical equations and the lookup table. It was found that as the flow velocity was faster, the fluid pressure increased, the spiral angle was larger, and the hydraulic diameter of the channel decreased, the measured friction coefficients generally became greater than those anticipated from the previous lookup table. It means that the previous equations and the lookup table for the friction coefficient may underestimate pressure loss in analyzing cooling channels. The present research only implies that there needs to be a fair margin in designing a cooling channel and estimating pressure loss from the previous empirical coefficients.
